1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
A lesson cannot help someone who cannot reach it.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,000
Many learners share one old phone, buy data in small bundles,
and often have no signal at home.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:18,000
And research shows that sending practice alone is not enough:
learners improve when a teacher follows up.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Lesson Parcel does both. The teacher writes a short lesson
in this web page.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:31,000
For each wrong answer, they can add a hint
about the mistake behind it.

6
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Download, and the whole lesson is one file of about 18 kilobytes.

7
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:43,000
It goes out in the class chat or by Bluetooth.
The learner switches on airplane mode.

8
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:49,000
The lesson still opens. It can read aloud,
and several learners can share one phone.

9
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:57,000
A wrong answer gets the hint for that exact answer,
not the solution. The explanation comes after a second try.

10
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:04,000
At the end: a first-try score,
and practice for the questions they missed.

11
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:10,000
High contrast and larger text are one tap away,
and the lesson is built for keyboards and screen readers.

12
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Then the learner gets a short code
and sends it by plain SMS. No data needed.

13
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:25,000
The teacher pastes the messages. Typos are caught,
and the class table shows what was hard.

14
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:36,000
Then the follow-up plan: learners grouped by the wrong answer
they chose, each group with a one-SMS message about its own mistake.

15
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:44,000
Copy, send, done. The teacher knows whom to help,
and with what.

16
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Lesson Parcel: one lesson, one tiny file,
and a way back to the teacher.

17
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:58,000
Free, open source, and tested offline.
